  "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the weight of the chocolate cookie, the oatcake, and the macaroon, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(10,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(15,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(14,1.5).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the difference between the sum of $6$ times the weight of the cookie and $5$ times the weight of the oatcake, and $10$ times the weight of the macaroon. Then, we have $Y=6{X}_{1}+5{X}_{2}-10{X}_{3},$ and we want to find \n\n$$\nP(Y<0).\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15-10\\cdot 14) \\\\  & =-5 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}\\cdot 1+{5}^{2}\\cdot 2+{10}^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =36+50+150 \\\\  & =236.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(-5,236).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y<0),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<0) & =P(Z<\\frac{0-(-5)}{\\sqrt{236}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<0) & ≈Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈0.6293.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
